"source_pdf_sha256":"ab3a5139bac83f192ad67529368d77b84b0d807e95a8e4fd0daa8d45fd046bec","page_index":29,"region":{"x1":87.84,"y1":107.04,"x2":528.48,"y2":265.92},"caption_region":{"x1":91.8010025024414,"y1":290.2535400390625,"x2":520.2027587890625,"y2":362.0099487304687},"caption":"Figure 16: Generalization gaps for different optimization methods. Following Wan et al. (2024), we visualize the generalization gap (i.e., the difference between final test set performance and the best achieved validation performance) for different optimizers. While Wan et al. (2024) previously observed that exemplars tend to generalize better, our results suggest that instructions generated by reflective prompt evolution can achieve stronger generalization as well as improved overall performance. We hypothesize this difference may be due to the improving capabilities of the underlying LLMs, as more recent models are both better at adhering to instructions and capable of reflecting on their outputs.","image_text":["Generalization","Gap","(Testset","vs.","Best","Validation","Score)","on","PUPA","for","Qwen3","8B","Final","Test","Score","Best","Validation","Score","Baseline","MIPROv2","GEPA","GEPA+Merge","-1.72%","1.46%","-2.40%","0.54%","e","Sc","or","92","90","88","86","84","82","80","78","Baseline","MIPROv2","GEPA","GEPA+Merge","-0.67%","-0.66%","Generalization","Gap","(Testset","vs.","Best","Validation","Score)","on","HoVer","for","Qwen3","8B","Final","Test","Score","Best","Validation","Score","Baseline","MIPROv2","GEPA","GEPA+Merge","2.33%","0.33%","e","Sc","or","52.5","50.0","47.5","45.0","42.5","40.0","37.5","35.0","Baseline","MIPROv2","GEPA","GEPA+Merge","2.33%","Generalization","Gap","(Testset","vs.","Best","Validation","Score)","on","HotpotQA","for","Qwen3","8B","Final","Test","Score","Best","Validation","Score","Baseline","MIPROv2","GEPA","GEPA+Merge","2.33%","-2.34%","-4.00%","e","Sc","or","60","55","50","45","Baseline","MIPROv2","GEPA","GEPA+Merge","-1.14%","Generalization","Gap